Two units, pick one. Enabling both makes them fight over the web port and the
rig's serial port.

wfweb.service -- user unit (systemctl --user). It runs inside your session,
next to PipeWire, so wfweb and other programs share the rig's USB CODEC.
Installed to lib/systemd/user:

    systemctl --user enable --now wfweb
    sudo loginctl enable-linger $USER

Without linger the unit lives only while you are logged in: it starts at your
first login (desktop or SSH) and stops when the last session closes. With
linger it starts at boot and stays up.

At boot wfweb and PipeWire start together. wfweb waits (up to 10 s) for
PipeWire to list the rig's CODEC instead of taking the raw device, so the
order they come up in does not matter.

wfweb@.service -- system unit, starts at boot, for a machine where wfweb is
the only program using the rig. The instance name is the account to run as;
do not use root. It has no session, so wfweb opens the raw hw: device and no
other program can use the rig's audio. Installed to lib/systemd/system:

    sudo systemctl enable --now wfweb@$USER
